            <ArticleTitle>The St. Leger total knee replacement: a 10-year clinical and radiological assessment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>322-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.knee.2009.02.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The St. Leger total knee replacement (Zynergy Orthopaedics Ltd, Rotherham, UK) was developed as a cheaper alternative to similar implants of its time. Between October 1993 and June 1999, 144 St. Leger total knee replacements were implanted into 114 patients. Seventy-three patients (99 knees) were recalled for assessment (mean follow-up of 10.2 years). Eighteen patients had had their prostheses revised, 11 had died and 12 were lost to follow-up. Functional Score showed 90% poor results and the Objective Knee Score showed 31% poor results. Radiological assessment identified 12 arthroplasties that had failed and 58 that required close follow. Kaplan-Meier cumulative survivorship was 87% at 10 years. The St. Leger knee replacement did not perform as well as others of the same generation and was not worth the initial financial savings.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
